WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:18.730
PROFESSOR: Ladies and
gentlemen, our speaker

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.720
this afternoon is Professor
Morris Halle, an Institute

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.820
professor who has been
associated with linguistics,

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.030
now linguistics and
philosophy, and with RLE.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:35.760
He joined RLE, as I did when
Jerry Wiesner was directing it,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:44.220
1951.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.400
So I've known Morris since
somewhat before that.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:54.270
Over all of the years, I
think my favorite quotation

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:59.250
came from when he received
the Killian Award,

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.460
and talked about how he
had become a linguist,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.110
and said that in his
childhood in Latvia,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.500
he grew up in a household in
which all the adults spoke

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.990
different-- every adult
had a different language.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:18.030
And he knew, he induced, being
an intelligent fellow, induced

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.040
from his surroundings
that when you grew up

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.690
you got your own language.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.250
And indeed it
happened, but he never

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.958
guessed that his was
going to be English.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.490
He's going to talk to us on
a representation of words

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:29.990
and memory.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.160
HALLE: You need a
handout anybody here

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.550
you can't follow this without--

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:47.480
this speech without a handout.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:02:25.360
Where's my-- Yeah,
that's better.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.060
The light is so strong.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:43.020
In speaking, as well as in
listening to speech of others.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.270
we have the very clear
and distinct impression

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:54.090
that all utterances are
composed of discrete words.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.360
Many people are quite surprised
when it's pointed out to them

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.960
that utterances are
quasi-continuous acoustically,

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:09.250
and normally contain no
pauses separating one

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.510
word from the next, the next.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:17.140
[LAUGHS]

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:22.640
And this impression of speaking
and hearing discrete words

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.640
in the continuous
speech signal is not

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.740
lost, even by the most
experienced speech researchers,

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:35.210
who are completely aware that
utterances are not acoustically

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:39.370
segmented into words.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.660
Now there's more to it.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.180
When I produce the
English utterance,

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.770
a dog never plays
when he's alone,

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:54.300
everyone in this room cannot
only repeat it, but when I ask,

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.040
can easily establish that the
utterances consisted of eight

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:06.160
words, a dog never
plays when he is alone.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.370
On the other hand, when
I produce the utterance

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:16.769
[NON-ENGLISH PHRASE],,
everyone in this room,

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:19.250
most people in this audience
will have difficulty

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.690
and repeating what I said and
will find it almost impossible

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:29.920
to determine how many words this
utterance was composed of, yet

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.340
you heard it just as clearly
as the other utterance I made.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.960
Well, there's of
course no mystery

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.450
about the result of
this little experiment.

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:41.680
The second utterance
was in Czech, a language

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.500
that I suppose not many
people in this room command.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:52.925
And since they did not
know Czech, they couldn't--

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:55.300
they didn't know the words--
since they don't know Czech,

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.650
they don't know
the words of Czech,

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:58.370
and knowledge of a
language, of course,

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:02.690
crucial to all
aspects of speech.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.730
Our experiment also
brings out the fact

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:08.810
that an essential aspect of
the knowledge of language

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.260
is the knowledge of your words.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.350
Now, most people think that
that's all, but it's not true.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.860
But I will not enlighten
you on that topic.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.350
I will limit--

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:23.630
I will perhaps reinforce
the incorrect impression

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:26.780
that many of you may have,
that knowing the words is all.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:32.010
It's a large part of what you
have to do, but it isn't all.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.310
Now, this totally unsurprising
proposition however,

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:38.180
has a number of corollaries
that provide helpful hints

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:41.090
about the nature of
language perhaps the most

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:45.590
important of these for our topic
is that we are not born knowing

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:48.160
the words of our language.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.700
The words we know we have
learned from our parents,

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:55.230
from other people, from
books or other media,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:59.160
so what does it
mean to know a word?

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.870
When we learn or
come to know a word,

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.380
we store in our
memory information

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.890
of a certain kind that
enables us to use the word

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.920
and not to insist that
we ourselves produce,

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:18.040
and to recognize it when used
in an utterance by someone else.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.580
The information that we
need in order to do this

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.760
is of two kinds.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.350
On the one hand,
we need information

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.940
about the sound of the word.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:29.877
Let us call this the
phonetic information.

00:06:29.877 --> 00:06:31.460
On the other hand,
we need information

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:35.160
about the meaning of the word,
about its lexical category,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.950
for example, we need
to know is it a noun

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:41.420
or a verb or an adjective,
it's grammatical peculiarities,

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.660
for example, is it a
strong or weak verb?

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:49.520
Does it form its plural with a
regular S as in cakes or keys

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.900
or with an N as in
children and oxen.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.610
Let's call the
latter information

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:58.820
grammatical semantic
information.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.480
The present talk will be limited
to the phonetic information.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.630
I shall have nothing
to say, next to nothing

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:07.043
to say, about the other kind.

00:07:07.043 --> 00:07:08.585
So if anybody wants
to talk about it,

00:07:08.585 --> 00:07:12.390
I'm happy to answer
questions about it.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:15.350
OK, so now we know
that we must store

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:22.610
in our memories, the phonetic
information about every word.

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.800
Now, this-- that is, we
are not born knowing it

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.580
and if you want to say
dog or cat or table

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:32.240
or whatever you want to
happen to want to say,

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.750
something has to be in
your memory to say it.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:40.490
We very rarely make up words.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.050
For all practical
purposes, we could

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:43.340
say we never make up words.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:46.680
We always get them
from the outside.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.960
Now this naturally
leads to the question

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.350
as to the form in
which this memory is

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.320
stored, the form in
which this information is

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.180
stored in our memory.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:59.600
It has been assumed by most
linguists and other students

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.620
of language that the phonetic
information about each word

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:09.020
is stored as a sequence of
discrete units called phonemes.

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.020
As a first approximation,
we may think of the phonemes

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:13.580
as the units that
are represented

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:16.130
by the letters in
written language.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.620
Thus the claim
that is being made

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:23.360
here is that speakers of
English remember the word cat

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.910
as being composed
of three phonemes,

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:29.330
whereas the word scat is a
sequence of four phonemes,

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:31.170
et cetera, et cetera.

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:34.400
If you reflect on it
a bit, the proposition

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.169
that the words are
composed of phonemes

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:38.659
is anything but self-evident.

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:41.780
It implies that all
speakers of all languages

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:44.930
store the words they know as
sequences of discrete letter

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:47.080
size units.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:50.470
Perhaps the most dubious
implication of this proposition

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.800
is that this must be true also
very young children, say, three

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.560
or four years and
younger, for many of them

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.250
speak quite fluently.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:00.990
If our claim is
correct, they must

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.770
be able not only to split
up into discrete words,

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.950
the continuous speech
that they hear,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.380
but they must also
be able to segment

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.200
the words into phonemes.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.900
Such children must
therefore be able to perform

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:19.380
sophisticated analysis
on the utterances they

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:24.190
hear that are beyond present day
capabilities of speech science.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:27.880
Even worse, perhaps
even worse, is

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.790
that there is nothing out there
in the world that might suggest

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:34.870
to two-year-old, or to
anyone for that matter,

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:36.970
that speech consists
of words and that words

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.060
are composed of phonemes.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.410
You know, this
discovery that the words

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:45.190
are composed of phonemes
underlies alphabetic writing.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:48.690
And alphabetic writing wasn't
discovered till, you know,

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:49.860
3,000 years ago.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.610
But human beings have
been around probably

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:56.760
speaking for God knows,
maybe a million years.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.820
But anyway, it's a
relatively recent discovery

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.170
that sort of was a
conscious discovery.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:05.900
Yet, as an
unconscious discovery,

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.340
if this claim is true, it
must underlie all language

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:10.985
at all times.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:19.010
And this is rather surprising,
and from self-evident.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:23.510
So how come that
somebody learns it,

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:26.540
since you know your mother
never tells you to study

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:27.990
the phonemes of your language.

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:31.750
And certainly mothers,
maybe now, recently,

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:34.700
that they become more anxious
about their children getting

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:36.020
into kindergarten.

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:39.740
But even when I
was a child, people

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:41.790
didn't worry too much about it.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.180
So the question is, how
come that children even

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:49.290
begin to get the idea
that they have to do that.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:56.210
Well, when you are faced
with unanswerable questions,

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.480
the obvious move, there
is one standard and moves

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:02.750
that every professor,
I mean everybody knows,

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:05.900
and that is to question
the presuppositions.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:07.970
So instead of
answering the question,

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.370
we all say, well, what's
the presupposition

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:11.200
of this question?

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.340
And maybe we can then get on.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.880
Now implicit in the subject
in this question and objection

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:20.160
is the assumption that
children need to learn both

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:21.830
that there are words
and that the words

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:24.110
are composed of phonemes.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:26.750
Instead, we might assume
something different.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:29.840
And here we follow
a line of reasoning

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:34.280
that I like lots of
what goes on here,

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.990
was pioneered by
Chomsky originally,

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:41.120
and namely that this knowledge
is hardwired into all of us

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.020
at birth.

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:46.610
And that the knowledge that
speech is composed of phonemes

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:49.790
is as much part of our
physiological makeup

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:53.270
as our heart or lungs and liver.

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:56.580
Our language has a word
for such innate knowledge.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.310
And that's the word instinct.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.790
And here I quote from
the Webster's third,

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:07.470
and it said that the instinct is
a complex and specific response

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:11.880
on the part of an organism
to environmental stimuli that

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:16.110
is largely hereditary and
unalterable, though the pattern

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.780
of behaviors through
which it is expressed

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:22.830
may be modified by learning,
that does not involve reason,

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.720
a learning that does
not involve reason,

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.290
and that has as its goal
the removal of attention

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:29.610
or excitation.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:34.860
Well, I mean if you have the
instinct for chopping words

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:36.800
into phonemes, well,
then you do them.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.070
There is nothing to explain.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:42.060
That's the only way you
can be because that's

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:44.400
what it means to be,
at least in part,

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.000
be human, and have the
capacity to learn language.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.130
So children now, if this
assumption is correct,

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:56.340
learn words not because
they're taught to do so,

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:59.310
but rather because they are
constructed from birth so as

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:00.100
to do this.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.700
And part of their net
machinery consists

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:06.090
of the mechanisms that analyze
words into its phonemes.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.990
And of course, we do
not know very much

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.280
about these mechanisms.

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:16.130
But that's the logic
of the situation.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:19.920
Now, there's even some very
simple experimental evidence

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:23.160
showing that very young English
speaking children analyze

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:25.350
their words at the phonemes.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.810
The evidence involves
the ability of children

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:31.620
to form plural self
nouns they know.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:35.670
So in English, the plurals
are formed, as you all know,

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.680
by adding S.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:39.250
Well, that's not quite correct.

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.280
If you look at
the examples in 2,

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:45.300
you will see that
they differ somewhat.

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.370
So, it's very clear,
if you look in,

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:52.446
let's say, 2C, that you don't
add just S. You add B CH.

00:13:52.446 --> 00:13:55.620
The word beach and the
plural of that is not beach,

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.540
but it's B ches.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:59.340
You add a whole syllable.

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:08.670
And if you looking 2B, you
don't add SS, which is, right?

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:11.610
But you add-- notice,
you don't say keys.

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:12.840
You don't say plays.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.920
You say keyz, playz, which is--

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:19.660
so you add zz there.

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:23.130
And finally, in fact,
you do add s, just

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:26.150
in the case of the
examples in 2A.

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:26.910
All right?

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:32.490
So, notice that there are three
distinct plurals, not just one.

00:14:35.858 --> 00:14:37.400
Those of you who
came in late, better

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:39.500
sit next to somebody
who has a handout.

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.390
I don't have any more.

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:43.390
Because you can't follow
this without the--

00:14:48.203 --> 00:14:48.703
OK.

00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:54.930
All right, so there's
three suffixes.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:58.010
And you know, the children
have to know which to say.

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:00.050
For example, if a
child says keys.

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:01.550
You'll notice that
there's something

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:03.320
wrong with the way
the child speaks

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:05.240
and Mom might get anxious.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.370
But children never do that.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:09.320
They always do--
they never say--

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:13.390
or if a child were to say
cakus, instead of cakes,

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:14.760
you would notice that.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:16.110
And children never do.

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:20.000
They know how to
say it correctly.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:24.890
In fact, it turns out that
these plural suffixes are not

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:26.240
randomly distributed.

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.430
You don't have to learn
each word with its plural.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:31.310
But there is a general rule.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:32.840
And a rule is given in--

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:36.080
one form of the rule, we'll
be talking about this rule,

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:37.860
is given in 3.

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:44.380
Now what it says there, if the
noun ends in chuh, juh, suh,

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:49.980
shuh, zshuh, then
the plural is us.

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:53.420
OK, that's the first
part of the rule.

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:58.040
Second part, otherwise, if
it doesn't end in this list,

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:06.890
then, if it ends in kuh, puh,
tuh, fuh, thuh, then add sss.

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:09.890
And finally, otherwise, add zzz.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:14.090
So you have like a decision
tree, you know check a list.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:15.680
You understand.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:16.410
OK?

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:19.190
Now, OK, so this is a rule.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:23.240
Now the question is, do
children know this rule

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:28.640
or is this something that's
clever linguists invented,

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:31.490
you know, people who
get PhDs, from MIT?

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:34.700
Well, the answer is that
children know this rule.

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:37.180
And in fact, in
the literature is

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:40.098
there's an experiment
that shows it.

00:16:40.098 --> 00:16:41.390
And that experiment is rather--

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:41.990
I'll tell you.

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.960
It is a rather
simple experiment.

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:51.770
The experimenters had
different kinds of dolls

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:53.330
and they gave them names.

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:57.620
So one doll had
the name was Clye.

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:02.420
And another doll was Clish.

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:06.630
Let's say another doll
was his name was Plick.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:10.329
And now, obviously these
are not English words.

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:12.560
The children had never
heard these words.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:17.099
The experimenters never-- so
they introduced these dolls

00:17:17.099 --> 00:17:21.540
to the children and then
they ran the experiments.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:25.619
And they were very careful never
to use plurals of these names.

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:28.800
And then they arranged them
in little groups in this room

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:31.080
where they the
test was conducted.

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:33.320
The children were brought in.

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:36.060
And the child was asked,
what's that, you pointing

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:39.720
at a group of dolls of
one type and a child would

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:42.300
say these are Clyes.

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:45.780
Or these are, what was it I had.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:47.190
Whatever it was.

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:48.800
I couldn't pass
that test anyway.

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:50.620
But they were--

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:53.310
I'm too old for that.

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:55.560
They said the correct plural.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:56.970
That is they always--

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:59.675
so for Clye, they said Clyes.

00:17:59.675 --> 00:18:01.310
For Clish, they said Clishes.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:03.420
And for Plick, they
said Plicks, you

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:06.720
know just like the way you're
supposed to say by this rule

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:07.290
number three.

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:12.850
Now notice, that they had
not heard these names before.

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:17.230
These names were
given right there.

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:19.050
That's the first time
they ever heard them.

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.150
And they had
certainly never heard

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:22.350
the plural of these words.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.350
But they knew how to do that.

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:28.030
Now, the conclusion
is, of course,

00:18:28.030 --> 00:18:29.980
that they knew the rule.

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:33.370
But knowing the rule implies
a little bit more than that.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:37.240
That is, only did they know
the rule, they could apply it.

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:40.510
And in order to apply the rule,
of course, what they had to do

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:46.060
is they had to slice the word
up into the last phoneme,

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:48.250
and the rest, minimally.

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:50.530
And then, once they had
done that, then they

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:54.760
could pick the right
suffix, namely,

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:57.250
whatever it was for
each of the three sets,

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:59.650
just as is given in
your rule number three.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:07.630
So the conclusion is
that none of the children

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:11.650
have knowledge of the kind
that presupposes their ability

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:15.640
to divide the continuous
speech signal up

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.400
into words, and the
words into phonemes,

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:24.260
and perform these
rather complicated,

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:26.600
perhaps not so
complicated, but anyway,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:30.340
these tasks of
making the plural.

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:34.030
Now the next question
I will briefly discuss

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:36.940
is what are phonemes?

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:40.450
Now, when you study
phonemes, I've

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:43.290
give you a list of
phonemes in four.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:47.760
Now, there's-- so puh,
buh, fuh, vuh, muh.

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.050
You know, those are the--

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:55.000
so pill, bill, phil, vale, mall,
or something like that, these

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:59.430
are the and similar
to duh and so on.

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:03.975
Now, when we pronounce these--

00:20:03.975 --> 00:20:05.350
as you study these
things, you'll

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:08.830
notice something that when
you pronounce these phonemes,

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:12.190
all the ones that I've given
you involve complete closure.

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:16.480
There's closure of your
mouth, so that the air

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:19.695
is obstructed in its outflow.

00:20:19.695 --> 00:20:21.070
That's very
different, let's say,

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:25.090
when you have other phonemes
like say, R, O, or E.

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:28.580
These are vowels and are quite
different from the consonants

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:30.100
that I've given
you, and they all

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:33.670
have this property
that somehow there's

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:38.920
an obstruction to
the flow of air.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:42.610
Now what differentiates
these three groups is

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:47.990
the piece of machinery that you
have that obstructs the flow.

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:54.910
So in the first set, puh, buh,
fuh, and so on, it's the lips.

00:20:54.910 --> 00:21:00.360
In the second set, it's the flat
part of the tongue, duh, duh.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:02.100
See, it's not the lips part.

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.590
It's the flat part
of the tongue.

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:09.520
And in the third set, guh, guh,
it's the body of the tongue

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:12.100
that that stops say the airflow.

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:20.270
So we have three groups,
depending on the articulator

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:21.110
is the class--

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:26.690
is the technical term that
interrupts the air flow.

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:31.970
So that's one property, that
the air flow is interrupted,

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:35.120
and there's a particular
articulator that

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:37.000
does the interrupting.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:38.765
And that's what
makes them different.

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:49.130
In the first line,
it says nasal.

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:54.230
What you notice there
is that we allow in some

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:57.110
sounds, the air to
flow through the nose.

00:21:57.110 --> 00:22:01.080
We open up a side
port as it were,

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:03.210
which allows the air to
flow through the nose

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:06.760
and that excites certain
resonances in these cavities

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:07.260
here.

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:09.535
And you have these
sounds like mmm,

00:22:09.535 --> 00:22:14.760
nnn, you know, these
so-called nasal sounds.

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.550
The rest of the sounds
are produced in such a way

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:21.930
that the soft
palate is raised up

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:26.310
and no air is allowed to
flow through the nose.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:27.930
And so, we have this--

00:22:31.490 --> 00:22:34.070
and you see, what you're
supposed to see here

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:37.250
is that this is a
particular gesture that

00:22:37.250 --> 00:22:40.070
is the same in all
three types of sound.

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:46.750
So nuh and muh are quite
similar in that you have

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:49.930
a lowering of this soft palate.

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:52.180
But in one case you
superimpose this

00:22:52.180 --> 00:22:54.850
on a labial stoppage of flow.

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:59.030
In the other case, you impose it
on a coronal stoppage of flow.

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:02.050
And in the third case on
a dorsal stoppage of flow.

00:23:06.174 --> 00:23:09.070
What you see here is
that they all, they

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:11.650
have a sort of similar
internal structure.

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:13.780
Now if you look at
the non-nasal sounds,

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:15.970
you notice two other things.

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:22.070
You notice that sounds
like puh and tuh and fuh

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:24.550
are produced without
vocal cord vibration.

00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:27.400
Now you can tell whether
your vocal cords vibrate.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:31.150
When you say zzzz, they vibrate.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:34.870
And you can feel that when you
say, ssss, they don't vibrate.

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:39.160
So there is another property
that this vocal cord

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:41.300
vibration that goes in.

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:44.970
And now you notice again, you
can vibrate your vocal cords

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:49.090
while stopping the
airflow with your lips,

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:53.320
or while stopping it
with your flat part

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:55.030
of the tongue or the
tongue body and you

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:58.070
can do that in different ways.

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:02.500
And finally, there-- and that
is represented by the minuses

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:04.220
and pluses in the second line.

00:24:04.220 --> 00:24:07.510
And it says, something
happens that your glottis,

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:11.390
that you switch on and off and
either the vocal cords vibrate

00:24:11.390 --> 00:24:12.150
or they don't.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:15.010
And that's represented
on line two.

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:19.300
Now finally, the
stoppage can be complete,

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:22.170
as it is, let's
say in puh, or tuh,

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:27.610
or it can be sort of partial as
it is, in let's say, th or sss,

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:31.800
where air is trapped inside
your mouth, but only partially.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:32.820
OK.

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:37.140
Now, and what you notice
here is that these

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:41.450
are sort of subgestures,
the closure, the lowering

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:49.040
of the velum, the behavior of
the vocal folds, each of these

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:54.290
is represented in
three variance,

00:24:54.290 --> 00:24:56.720
in the first class, where
the major closure is made

00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:58.670
with the lips, in
second class where it's

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:00.530
made with the flat
part of the tongue,

00:25:00.530 --> 00:25:07.460
in the third part where it's
performed with the tongue body,

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:09.380
by raising the tongue body.

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:15.290
And now these subgestures,
you find in all--

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:19.970
each phonemes therefore is sort
of a complex of subgestures.

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:22.670
And the technical term
is to say that phonemes

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:25.580
are complexes of features.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:28.490
Now, so that a
speech sound is not

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:33.330
something which is further
unanalyzable, but rather it's

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:37.650
a complex of subproperties.

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:41.840
So now, and perhaps the
most interesting observation

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:44.420
to be made is, of all
the sounds in the world,

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:49.490
only phonemes are
complexes of features

00:25:49.490 --> 00:25:56.540
and only can be
analyzed into phonemes.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:59.530
And they must be
analyzed into phonemes.

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:04.060
So when you hear,
to ask, what does--

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:12.550
when you represent, let's
say a sound of the car

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:15.640
or a rattle in your
car, you don't have

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.830
phonemes to represent that.

00:26:17.830 --> 00:26:20.410
I mean some kind
of rattle that--

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:26.200
whereas words, they
divided into phonemes.

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.330
And the phonemes are
made up of features.

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:29.360
That's sort of the idea.

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:30.130
OK.

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:33.010
Now this digression, this
technical digression,

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:36.910
was necessary to bring
out the next thing.

00:26:36.910 --> 00:26:40.000
Now obviously, none
of you, or most of you

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:44.080
who has never heard of
phonemes before, now I'm

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:46.060
here the first to tell you.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:53.950
Now what's interesting is that
we now may ask a question.

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:57.070
Do people who know
English do they think

00:26:57.070 --> 00:27:02.650
about the phonemes in
terms of indivisible units

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:11.440
or our English speakers,
in their naive behavior,

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:13.990
is their behavior
properly characterized

00:27:13.990 --> 00:27:15.230
in terms of features?

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:16.950
And here is an--

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:19.910
and we will try to show
you how that works.

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:24.580
Now, if the latter is
true, then the knowledge

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:29.860
is that the people that
know the plural rule, that

00:27:29.860 --> 00:27:34.200
know the rule number
three, then of course, they

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:36.510
wouldn't have lists of phonemes.

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:41.250
They would have feature
lists of some sort.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:43.620
That is, this
knowledge would not

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:47.070
be represented by
a list of phonemes.

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:50.190
But if phonemes are
always feature bundles,

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:53.550
then they would be
represented by feature vowels.

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:57.840
So for example, let's
now look at five,

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:03.390
which is a translation
of three into features.

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:10.550
Now notice, the first set is
the sounds suh, zjuh, suh, zuh,

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:11.910
chuh, juh.

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:17.170
Now, all these sounds are
made with the flat part

00:28:17.170 --> 00:28:17.760
of the tongue.

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:19.970
They are coronal.

00:28:19.970 --> 00:28:23.260
They have the coronal feature.

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:25.240
And moreover, they have
some other features

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:26.840
which we won't bother.

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:33.500
OK, so the translation of the
list, that's the list in 3A,

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:37.370
is, as I've given in five,
saying it's cornonal.

00:28:37.370 --> 00:28:42.840
Does it have the feature coronal
plus some other features?

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:47.530
Now the second list says,
puh, tuh, kuh, fuh, thuh.

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:50.490
Now, those are all the
sounds and only that

00:28:50.490 --> 00:28:53.510
are made out without
vibration of the vocal folds.

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:56.340
They're unvoiced.

00:28:56.340 --> 00:29:00.960
And so the rule now has been
translated into into feature.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:04.950
I mean five is just a
translation into feature terms,

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:09.290
of that list that I've
given you in three.

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:11.550
And now the question
is, in which

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:17.570
form do you, as a speaker of
English, know the plural rule?

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:21.510
Now the answer, and we'll now
run an experiment right away,

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:24.010
and we will see
whether it works.

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:28.370
OK, now the test
was suggested to me

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:31.310
many years ago by
at Lise Menn, who's

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:34.370
now a professor at the
University of Colorado.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:38.905
And what the test that she
suggests was quite simple.

00:29:38.905 --> 00:29:40.280
First of all, she
said, look, you

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:47.197
can ask people to make
plurals of non-English words.

00:29:47.197 --> 00:29:49.280
So for example, if you
knew a Frenchman whose name

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:54.500
was de la Rue, although [? Uu ?]
doesn't exist in English,

00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:58.030
you could ask what's the
plural of Monsieur de La

00:29:58.030 --> 00:29:59.400
Rue and his family.

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:02.240
And somebody would--

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:03.320
So what is it?

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:04.160
Which one?

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:05.720
Which one?

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:06.420
Which one?

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:11.280
Well, which one, the first,
the second, or the third?

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:12.800
Third one OK, fine.

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:13.300
Good.

00:30:13.300 --> 00:30:14.420
We're getting there.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:14.920
Now.

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:27.620
The two lists make
different predictions

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:31.490
about this sound, oo.

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:33.530
Number three says,
regardless, of what

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:36.260
the sound is, if it's
not an English sound,

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:38.590
you have to make zzz.

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:40.950
That's what number three says.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:43.230
But number 5 doesn't say that.

00:30:43.230 --> 00:30:47.060
Number five says,
look at its features.

00:30:47.060 --> 00:30:52.010
If it has the features,
then apply the rule.

00:30:52.010 --> 00:30:57.970
OK, so now we have to
find a phoneme of English,

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:01.120
which not in English, but
which has appropriate features.

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:04.570
So that wouldn't
pick number three.

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:05.740
And we do have that.

00:31:05.740 --> 00:31:09.530
And everybody knows
Johann Sebastian Bach.

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:15.980
Now Bach, he had many children.

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:21.540
And so, what's the
plural of Bach.

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:24.240
Number two, not number three.

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:25.420
OK?

00:31:25.420 --> 00:31:28.500
Now it's clear that everybody
picks number two and not

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:29.910
number three.

00:31:29.910 --> 00:31:34.080
Although chuh is not
a phoneme of English.

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:36.330
Therefore there
couldn't be a list.

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:37.800
It couldn't be
listed in anything

00:31:37.800 --> 00:31:39.330
that's a rule of English.

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:43.200
And therefore, since the
action was performed here

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:45.570
and the test was
run here, it's clear

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:50.230
that somehow or other people
know something about features.

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:51.820
Maybe they had never
heard about it.

00:31:51.820 --> 00:31:53.670
But then you know
a lot of things

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:59.300
you haven't heard about that
play a real role in life.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:31:59.800
OK.

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:06.170
So we have now established
that phonemes are actually

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:07.790
bundles of features.

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:13.550
And that speakers have
knowledge in a very strong sense

00:32:13.550 --> 00:32:14.990
of these features.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:17.370
They may not have any
way of talking about it.

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:20.870
But then we don't have any way
of talking about how we walk.

00:32:20.870 --> 00:32:23.720
We don't know how
we balance ourselves

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:26.360
so that we don't fall down.

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:30.170
But we know how to do that even
though we can't talk about it.

00:32:30.170 --> 00:32:30.670
OK.

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:38.770
So these are features.

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:42.530
I mean that phoneme is
a bundle of features.

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:45.080
And a word, now, we
can now say something

00:32:45.080 --> 00:32:48.590
about the way the word
is represented in memory,

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:53.330
namely be represented by a
matrix, where each column will

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:55.070
represent a column of features.

00:32:55.070 --> 00:32:58.160
And there will be
as many phonemes--

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:00.110
each phoneme will be
a column of features.

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:03.470
And there'll be as many phonemes
in the word as there are--

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:07.230
as many columns as there
are phonemes in the word.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:09.370
So that's now the
claim that we're

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:13.460
going to make about the
representation of words

00:33:13.460 --> 00:33:14.035
in memory.

00:33:14.035 --> 00:33:15.410
Now we want to go
on a little bit

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:18.930
and say perhaps a
little bit more.

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:26.010
Now, notice that the
features are, in one sense,

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:27.270
they are instructions.

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:34.380
They are instructions
for actions.

00:33:34.380 --> 00:33:37.230
That is in one case,
say lower your velum.

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:40.140
Or close your lips.

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:45.460
Or they are actions that
are observable actions.

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:48.340
Now in memory of course,
these instructions

00:33:48.340 --> 00:33:52.360
serve to distinguish
one word from another.

00:33:52.360 --> 00:33:55.210
So the difference between, let's
say, the word bill and the word

00:33:55.210 --> 00:33:59.950
mill, is that in mill, we
have the instruction lower

00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:02.260
your velum, plus nasal.

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:06.980
Whereas in bill,
it's minus nasal.

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:09.290
That is the instruction.

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:17.090
But in memory they serve as a
kind of a purely at diacritic

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:20.810
m which distinguishes
one matrix from another.

00:34:20.810 --> 00:34:24.020
And only at some
particular point

00:34:24.020 --> 00:34:30.030
does it eventuate in action.

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:34.909
Now interestingly,
when you hear a signal,

00:34:34.909 --> 00:34:39.800
you therefore have
to analyze the signal

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:42.290
in terms of these
actions in order

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:46.469
to pick the word out of your
memory if the story is correct.

00:34:46.469 --> 00:34:49.280
I mean, the matrices
are matrices

00:34:49.280 --> 00:34:52.449
in terms of actions
of particular pieces

00:34:52.449 --> 00:34:53.960
of the machinery here.

00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:59.540
Now so what this sort of
presupposes or implies

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:03.350
is that there is a kind of
a primacy of the gymnastics

00:35:03.350 --> 00:35:06.950
that's going on over the
sound that's out there.

00:35:06.950 --> 00:35:11.690
That is, you analyze a sound
in terms of the gymnastics

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:16.070
in order to figure
out which word did he

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:19.430
say in order for me to
understand that person whose

00:35:19.430 --> 00:35:22.280
speech I'm now understanding?

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:26.150
Now this may sound
rather surprising.

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:29.000
Now some researchers
have questioned

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:32.780
the need for the
translation from acoustics

00:35:32.780 --> 00:35:34.820
into articulations.

00:35:34.820 --> 00:35:37.270
Articulations is
this gymnastics.

00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:40.250
And I've proposed instead
that features in memory

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:42.860
are directly related
to properties

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:47.000
in the acoustic signal, rather
than being indirectly related

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:49.120
to properties in
the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:51.200
It is therefore
necessary at this point

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:53.990
to review the
evidence that supports

00:35:53.990 --> 00:35:56.360
the alternative view that--

00:35:56.360 --> 00:35:58.870
for me, since I
have proposed that,

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:01.650
that the acoustic signal
is not directly related

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:04.610
to the specific acoustic
properties and in the signal,

00:36:04.610 --> 00:36:06.620
but rather that
this relationship is

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:09.720
indirect mediated.

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:12.700
Now this view, by the way,
has a name in the literature.

00:36:12.700 --> 00:36:14.820
And it's called
the motor theory.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:18.450
And it has been
championed for a long time

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:21.870
by Alan Liberman of
Haskins Laboratory

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:25.680
and a number of other
people that you may know.

00:36:25.680 --> 00:36:32.160
Now I will give a
little bit of evidence

00:36:32.160 --> 00:36:34.050
why I think this
is so, that there's

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:38.730
a primacy of the
articulation over the sound.

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:42.930
And the first
evidence is, when you

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:49.900
ask speakers of any
language to say a word,

00:36:49.900 --> 00:36:55.050
so you make a nonsense
word like soup, let's say,

00:36:55.050 --> 00:36:56.360
just made up some word.

00:36:56.360 --> 00:36:59.820
And you ask somebody to say
it, without even in the dark,

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:02.910
so they can't see or
you play a record,

00:37:02.910 --> 00:37:04.830
so they can't see what
you do when you say,

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:08.220
please repeat this word, the
actions that they perform

00:37:08.220 --> 00:37:09.960
will be enormously uniform.

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:11.610
They may not 100%.

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:14.208
But the uniformity is
enormously surprising.

00:37:14.208 --> 00:37:15.750
So when they hear
that word they will

00:37:15.750 --> 00:37:19.290
start by raising the
flat part of the tongue

00:37:19.290 --> 00:37:20.560
against the roof of the mouth.

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:23.070
And around their
lips, and then they

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:24.950
raise the body of the tongue.

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:27.510
The actions are executed
always the same.

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:31.620
There will never be an
attempt to mimic the sound

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:33.450
by putting your
fingers in your mouth

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:35.190
or doing something desperate.

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:39.600
You know, they will
always be the uniform.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:42.090
And this is universal.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:46.440
I mean there is no instruction,
no teaching, that happens

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:47.880
when you make such a request.

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:52.530
And so it's very it's not
only in English speaking,

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:55.380
when you ask somebody who never
spoke and never heard English,

00:37:55.380 --> 00:37:57.060
tell them to
pronounce this sound

00:37:57.060 --> 00:38:00.170
and there will be an
enormous uniformity.

00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:01.730
And the uniformity
will be in terms

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:07.220
of is this execution of
this particular action.

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:10.100
Now notice, when you ask
people to produce a sound,

00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:12.860
you make, and you
make some odd sound.

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:15.280
And say, well, see
what you can do.

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:18.620
And you know, like scratching
two pieces of metal plates

00:38:18.620 --> 00:38:20.420
and reproduce this sound.

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:22.850
And people get very lost.

00:38:22.850 --> 00:38:25.040
They're very flustered.

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:26.480
We have this built in.

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:30.590
Certain sounds, we analyze
into articulatory gestures,

00:38:30.590 --> 00:38:31.530
namely features.

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:32.870
And those we then produce.

00:38:32.870 --> 00:38:36.380
And then others we do
all kinds of wild things.

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:40.090
Another bit of evidence is the
so-called McGurk experiment

00:38:40.090 --> 00:38:42.330
as some of you may know.

00:38:42.330 --> 00:38:45.380
Now the McGurk
experiment consisted

00:38:45.380 --> 00:38:53.210
of the following
rather surprising test.

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:59.090
People heard a tape
and shown a tape

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:03.860
in a video where somebody says,
da, da, da, da, da, da, da.

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:13.170
At a particular point, the
audio portion remains the same,

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:19.150
but the video portion changes to
somebody saying, like this, you

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:22.710
know, Saying doo, doo,
doo, doo essentially,

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:26.120
although the audio portion
continues saying da, da, da.

00:39:26.120 --> 00:39:28.100
And the impression
that people have

00:39:28.100 --> 00:39:30.440
is that the person switched.

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:32.590
And that he is now
saying doo, doo.

00:39:32.590 --> 00:39:35.480
And you can actually
turn off the video

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:37.360
and they hear the
da, da, ad again.

00:39:37.360 --> 00:39:41.330
And you turn the video up,
and it's serving the illusion.

00:39:41.330 --> 00:39:42.950
And the illusion
simply shows you

00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:52.460
that are the
articulatory information,

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:55.380
once you receive this
articulatory cue,

00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:58.040
I mean, so overwhelming,
that you think that you

00:39:58.040 --> 00:39:59.250
hear something different.

00:39:59.250 --> 00:40:02.690
So here's another bit of
some of kind of evidence

00:40:02.690 --> 00:40:04.490
that suggests that.

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:07.130
Now the third is what
happens in language.

00:40:07.130 --> 00:40:11.060
And thus is this my last point
I will take about 10 minutes

00:40:11.060 --> 00:40:11.570
to make it.

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:20.360
And that is that, in
addition to making plurals,

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:23.300
there are other kind
of rules in language.

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:25.310
And I'll begin by one.

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:28.820
And these rules as you will
see, always affect features.

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:32.480
They always affect one
or two features as you

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:33.810
will see in just a moment.

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:38.770
So take for example,
the words in six.

00:40:38.770 --> 00:40:44.560
Now notice, when we say,
take a word like leaf.

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:51.560
Now the plural of leaf
happens not to be leafs,

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:53.740
but it's leaves.

00:40:53.740 --> 00:40:56.450
We say chief, chiefs.

00:40:56.450 --> 00:40:59.030
But leaf, leaves.

00:40:59.030 --> 00:40:59.900
OK.

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:02.930
Now the same thing,
we'll take house.

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:07.710
The plural of house
is not houses.

00:41:07.710 --> 00:41:10.440
That would be the plural
of Hermans and his family.

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:14.210
But it would be it's houses.

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:18.330
I mean so what happens
is that in the plural,

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:24.040
we change the shape of the stem.

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:27.465
The noun in the plural
has a different sound

00:41:27.465 --> 00:41:28.340
than in the singular.

00:41:28.340 --> 00:41:31.390
So house in the plural
in the singular.

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:36.080
But houzz in the plural.

00:41:36.080 --> 00:41:38.540
How we don't in--

00:41:38.540 --> 00:41:39.830
not reflected in the spelling.

00:41:39.830 --> 00:41:44.000
But notice for f and
v we reflect that even

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:44.690
in the spelling.

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:47.900
So we say leaf, but
the plural is leaves

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:50.570
and we write a different
letter to reflect that.

00:41:50.570 --> 00:41:52.530
But for house we don't bother.

00:41:52.530 --> 00:41:54.915
We don't write z there.

00:41:54.915 --> 00:41:56.540
There's a small number
of English words

00:41:56.540 --> 00:41:59.206
which have this property.

00:41:59.206 --> 00:42:06.100
And In order to speak English
correctly, you have to do that.

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:08.710
If you said, leafs,
somebody would correct you.

00:42:08.710 --> 00:42:09.730
That's not correct.

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:15.160
I mean you so there is
this kind of small rule.

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:19.000
And we have a rule, which
I've written in number seven.

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:21.370
And what the rule
says is that if you

00:42:21.370 --> 00:42:24.770
have a sound such as plus
continuant means ffss,

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:29.290
it is that kind of sound,
which has incomplete blockage

00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:30.790
of the flow.

00:42:30.790 --> 00:42:35.440
This sound in the
plural becomes voiced.

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:39.010
It changes its composition
so that instead

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:42.370
of being produced without
vocal cord vibration,

00:42:42.370 --> 00:42:45.780
it's now produced with
vocal cord vibration.

00:42:45.780 --> 00:42:49.370
So there is such-- and that's
part of knowing English.

00:42:49.370 --> 00:42:53.400
OK, now, interestingly,
there's other things

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:54.900
that affect voicing in English.

00:42:54.900 --> 00:42:57.960
So let's take the
suffix that's spelled

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:04.290
th, which you find on the one
hand in the ordinal numbers,

00:43:04.290 --> 00:43:06.760
so twelfths.

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:08.260
Twelfths.

00:43:08.260 --> 00:43:12.170
Twelve, but twelfths.

00:43:12.170 --> 00:43:18.740
Say, now, or, five,
and you say fifths.

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:22.460
It's not five, but fifth.

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:27.440
So there's, the vuh becomes
fuh, which in speech term,

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:30.610
means it becomes voiceless.

00:43:30.610 --> 00:43:35.350
And notice the same
thing happens in the--

00:43:35.350 --> 00:43:40.910
when the th is used to make
a noun from an adjective.

00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:46.554
So we say swide
And we say widths.

00:43:46.554 --> 00:43:53.690
duh, but widths Or we say,
or broad, we say breadths.

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:55.900
OK I mean there
other such examples.

00:43:55.900 --> 00:43:56.800
All right?

00:43:56.800 --> 00:44:02.860
Now what's interesting
is that here again, we

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:06.370
have, in terms of
features, what we would say

00:44:06.370 --> 00:44:14.200
is that this consonant becomes
minus voice before the suffix

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:15.420
th.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:21.620
You this like this
is a suffix th.

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:25.120
But here it's sort of
wrong to put it that way.

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:30.520
Because really what's going
on here is not this th

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:34.730
is itself minus voice.

00:44:34.730 --> 00:44:36.240
You know, when we had the--

00:44:36.240 --> 00:44:39.180
I've written here without
the feature composition.

00:44:39.180 --> 00:44:42.680
But th itself, th,
is a voiceless.

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:47.490
So now what's going on here is
that the feature minus voice,

00:44:47.490 --> 00:44:52.840
is sort of spread onto
the preceding sound.

00:44:52.840 --> 00:44:56.090
It's being anticipated,
as it were.

00:44:56.090 --> 00:44:58.740
See, it's not just any
change that's going on here.

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:01.560
It isn't as though you
know, this constant becomes

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:04.820
the nasal or some crazy change.

00:45:04.820 --> 00:45:07.170
The change here
is really a change

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:13.470
of a feature spreading from
one phoneme onto the other.

00:45:13.470 --> 00:45:15.300
And if what you
have here is a kind

00:45:15.300 --> 00:45:18.450
of an anticipation
of voicelessness.

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:20.710
You know that this is
going to be voiceless.

00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:24.780
So therefore, you
anticipate it already.

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:29.550
But now let's go back to
the English plural rule.

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:36.200
Notice what we had there was,
we had, we said, well, we get,

00:45:36.200 --> 00:45:37.610
if it's minus voice.

00:45:40.760 --> 00:45:46.750
then it becomes sss,
otherwise it's zzz.

00:45:46.750 --> 00:45:50.810
Remember that's what rule
five said, the last part.

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:52.870
But again, what
you have here, see,

00:45:52.870 --> 00:45:56.810
is a spreading in the
opposite direction.

00:45:56.810 --> 00:46:01.640
That is, the spreading it
goes from the end of the stem

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:07.280
onto the suffix, that
is the suffix was zz

00:46:07.280 --> 00:46:09.640
or the basic suffix is zz.

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:14.260
But if it follows a
voiceless consonant,

00:46:14.260 --> 00:46:17.490
then the voicing feature
now is spread onto it.

00:46:17.490 --> 00:46:22.090
So here you have as it were, a
kind of a perseverance inertia

00:46:22.090 --> 00:46:25.570
effect, where this
feature of the stem

00:46:25.570 --> 00:46:28.510
is held over onto the suffix.

00:46:28.510 --> 00:46:33.630
And I've represented these
two things in your handout

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:40.570
in, one is, in 10 and the
other one is in the 11.

00:46:40.570 --> 00:46:44.230
Where I try to show
you that in one case

00:46:44.230 --> 00:46:47.780
we have kind of an anticipation
effect, and in the other case,

00:46:47.780 --> 00:46:52.280
we have an inertia effect.

00:46:52.280 --> 00:47:00.590
And now again, is that I want
to go on and show you a very

00:47:00.590 --> 00:47:03.510
surprising, unexpected effect.

00:47:03.510 --> 00:47:07.180
Another is when we look
at the actual gesture

00:47:07.180 --> 00:47:09.760
and it begins to make
very good sense when

00:47:09.760 --> 00:47:11.260
we look at the
actual gesture, which

00:47:11.260 --> 00:47:13.320
I'm going to now tell you.

00:47:13.320 --> 00:47:16.220
OK, now how do you do--

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:17.870
how is voicing produced?

00:47:17.870 --> 00:47:20.180
I mean, we vibrate
our vocal cords.

00:47:20.180 --> 00:47:22.210
How we do that?

00:47:22.210 --> 00:47:26.900
Well, we don't-- each vibration
isn't individually controlled.

00:47:26.900 --> 00:47:27.980
They go much too fast.

00:47:27.980 --> 00:47:31.350
They go several
hundred times a second.

00:47:31.350 --> 00:47:35.250
So it has to be done
in some other way.

00:47:35.250 --> 00:47:38.840
And the way you do it
is basically ttthpt,

00:47:38.840 --> 00:47:40.080
like the soul.

00:47:40.080 --> 00:47:44.810
I mean what right it's a kind of
a relaxation oscillation, where

00:47:44.810 --> 00:47:48.120
you blow air up from the lungs.

00:47:48.120 --> 00:47:50.840
The vocal chords sit
on top of the lungs.

00:47:50.840 --> 00:47:55.360
They're elastic as the
pressure builds up.

00:47:55.360 --> 00:47:58.100
The vocal chords
are shoved apart.

00:47:58.100 --> 00:48:01.250
Then some air escapes.

00:48:01.250 --> 00:48:05.680
The pressure drops.

00:48:05.680 --> 00:48:07.270
The Bernoulli effect takes over.

00:48:07.270 --> 00:48:08.430
Vocal cords close.

00:48:08.430 --> 00:48:11.080
And you know the whole
thing starts all over again

00:48:11.080 --> 00:48:12.860
from the beginning.

00:48:12.860 --> 00:48:16.500
So that's the actual action.

00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:21.540
Now Ken Stevens and I,
who, like Peter Elias and I

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:33.870
go back to prehistoric times,
so we some 25 years ago we

00:48:33.870 --> 00:48:35.780
investigated the matter.

00:48:35.780 --> 00:48:39.130
We studied this whole
question of how that works.

00:48:39.130 --> 00:48:41.670
And one of the things
we noticed immediately,

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:45.120
and for this we claim no
credit, or we shouldn't claim

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:48.720
any credit, is that I
mean that what happens

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:51.460
is you stiffen the vocal cords.

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:56.650
As you stiffen the vocal
cords, what happens?

00:48:56.650 --> 00:48:58.600
Now interesting, the
first thing that happens

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:03.780
is that the rate of
vibration will increase.

00:49:03.780 --> 00:49:09.900
So as you make them stiffer,
the rate of vibration increases.

00:49:09.900 --> 00:49:12.540
Now we know that we control--

00:49:12.540 --> 00:49:18.250
what does the vocal
cord vibration reflect?

00:49:18.250 --> 00:49:20.660
Well, when I increase
my vocal cords--

00:49:20.660 --> 00:49:24.130
let's say when I say
a vowel, or any vowel,

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:26.690
and I increase the rate of
vibration of my vocal cord,

00:49:26.690 --> 00:49:28.300
the pitch goes up.

00:49:28.300 --> 00:49:30.980
I say, ah, oh, oh.

00:49:30.980 --> 00:49:33.620
These are changes in
the rate of vibration

00:49:33.620 --> 00:49:39.470
that are effective probably
by changing the stiffness

00:49:39.470 --> 00:49:42.840
of the vocal chords.

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:48.270
Now, however, and here's
the more interesting fact,

00:49:48.270 --> 00:49:51.600
when you make a
vowel sound, you're

00:49:51.600 --> 00:49:55.740
as I indicated were,
your mouth is wide open

00:49:55.740 --> 00:49:58.630
and no air is trapped
in your mouth.

00:49:58.630 --> 00:50:01.420
And the pressure
inside your mouth

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:03.940
is roughly ambient pressure.

00:50:03.940 --> 00:50:10.660
And so the drop from what
goes on from the lungs

00:50:10.660 --> 00:50:13.340
into the mouth is sort
of the maximal pressure

00:50:13.340 --> 00:50:16.220
that the difference that can be.

00:50:16.220 --> 00:50:22.350
Now what happens when you say,
let's say, sss or the zzz?

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:28.620
Well when you say sss, you
trap air in your mouth.

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:31.590
And some pressure is built
up inside your mouth.

00:50:31.590 --> 00:50:35.550
And therefore the drop
across the vocal folds,

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:38.730
assuming that the pressure in
the lungs remains the same,

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:40.450
is less.

00:50:40.450 --> 00:50:42.250
So we have two cases.

00:50:42.250 --> 00:50:47.550
In a sound like sss, we
have a small pressure drop.

00:50:47.550 --> 00:50:51.060
And in a sound like ahh, we
have maximum pressure drop.

00:50:51.060 --> 00:50:53.670
And now, the question
we asked, was

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:58.050
what happens as you change,
make the same changes

00:50:58.050 --> 00:51:00.990
in vocal chords stiffness?

00:51:00.990 --> 00:51:04.200
What will be the behavior
of the vocal chords?

00:51:04.200 --> 00:51:07.230
Now we know in the case
of big pressure drop,

00:51:07.230 --> 00:51:11.020
there will be increasing
in the rate of vibration.

00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:13.840
But in the case of a
little pressure drop,

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:17.230
small pressure drop, what
happens not surprisingly, when

00:51:17.230 --> 00:51:22.730
you think about it, is you
increase the stiffness,

00:51:22.730 --> 00:51:25.950
the vocal chords
stopped vibrating.

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:29.960
Because there's just not
enough push at this point to--

00:51:29.960 --> 00:51:33.150
the stiffness is now so
great that and the pressure

00:51:33.150 --> 00:51:37.320
is so small that they
simply don't vibrate.

00:51:37.320 --> 00:51:46.030
So that says that as a gesture,
the differences in pitch, that

00:51:46.030 --> 00:51:52.240
is high pitch versus low pitch,
and voicelessness, absence

00:51:52.240 --> 00:51:55.860
of vocal cord vibration,
and presence of vocal cord

00:51:55.860 --> 00:52:01.490
vibration, are produced
by the same mechanism.

00:52:01.490 --> 00:52:01.990
Right?

00:52:01.990 --> 00:52:05.650
That is the same
articulatory mechanism

00:52:05.650 --> 00:52:09.070
that is involved in
making changes in pitch

00:52:09.070 --> 00:52:12.520
is present in making the
difference-- differentiating

00:52:12.520 --> 00:52:16.050
sss from zzz from vvv and so on.

00:52:18.670 --> 00:52:21.250
Now that's simply the
logic of the situ.

00:52:21.250 --> 00:52:22.870
That seems to be the way it is.

00:52:22.870 --> 00:52:25.170
Now the question is, but now
notice what's interesting

00:52:25.170 --> 00:52:28.720
and if you look in number
in your handout under 13,

00:52:28.720 --> 00:52:31.970
I've sort of made a
table there, and I said,

00:52:31.970 --> 00:52:34.240
there's a large pressure drop.

00:52:34.240 --> 00:52:36.550
And on the left, there's
a stiff vocal folds

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:38.530
and slack vocal folds.

00:52:38.530 --> 00:52:42.340
And there was a large pressure
drop, higher rate of vibration

00:52:42.340 --> 00:52:47.080
versus lower rate of vibration,
with small pressure drop,

00:52:47.080 --> 00:52:50.080
absence of vibration,
cessation of vibration,

00:52:50.080 --> 00:52:52.610
presence of vibration.

00:52:52.610 --> 00:52:53.140
OK?

00:52:53.140 --> 00:52:55.910
So but now notice acoustically,
and here is a question

00:52:55.910 --> 00:52:57.230
that we try to raise.

00:52:57.230 --> 00:52:59.540
What has primacy?

00:52:59.540 --> 00:53:04.730
Is it the gymnastics
or is the sound?

00:53:04.730 --> 00:53:07.580
OK, now notice, the
sound is very different.

00:53:07.580 --> 00:53:10.460
I mean there is really
nothing in common

00:53:10.460 --> 00:53:15.410
between a high pitched vowel
and a voiceless consonant

00:53:15.410 --> 00:53:19.820
and a low pitched vowel
and a voice consonant.

00:53:19.820 --> 00:53:22.070
So here is--
acoustically as they

00:53:22.070 --> 00:53:24.530
should go their separate ways.

00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:26.680
But from the point of
view of the mechanism,

00:53:26.680 --> 00:53:30.608
they seem to be
going the same way.

00:53:30.608 --> 00:53:32.400
But we have even, when
we have a little bit

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:35.390
of linguistic evidence
to show that perhaps

00:53:35.390 --> 00:53:36.690
that's the right way to go.

00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:44.180
And that is and this evidence
is given in these examples

00:53:44.180 --> 00:53:51.110
from a Chinese
dialect in number 14.

00:53:51.110 --> 00:53:56.380
Now what goes on there
is, in the Chinese words,

00:53:56.380 --> 00:53:59.570
in many of the
Chinese languages,

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:04.460
they are differentiated by the
pitch contours that they have.

00:54:04.460 --> 00:54:07.760
So they have a falling
contour or a rising contour

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:09.110
or an even contour.

00:54:09.110 --> 00:54:12.760
And that makes
differences in meaning.

00:54:12.760 --> 00:54:14.370
I mean, you know
everybody knows that.

00:54:14.370 --> 00:54:18.483
OK, but now in this particular
dialect, which is not

00:54:18.483 --> 00:54:19.900
the standard, I
mean we should not

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:22.870
the Mandarin standard
dialect or any of the--

00:54:22.870 --> 00:54:25.380
it's a particular
[? Amoid ?] dialect.

00:54:25.380 --> 00:54:27.580
Now what you see there is this.

00:54:27.580 --> 00:54:30.620
That look on the
left hand column.

00:54:30.620 --> 00:54:34.060
Now the numbers
indicate pitches.

00:54:34.060 --> 00:54:37.155
So five is the highest
pitch and one is the lowest.

00:54:37.155 --> 00:54:42.730
So five three is a falling pitch
and three five is a rising.

00:54:42.730 --> 00:54:44.740
And four four is even.

00:54:44.740 --> 00:54:48.070
But now notice, you have
these two sets of words.

00:54:48.070 --> 00:54:53.870
And they have falling,
even, and rising.

00:54:53.870 --> 00:54:59.240
And they come in two versions,
a high pitch version,

00:54:59.240 --> 00:55:04.560
when the sound is tuh,
and a low pitched version

00:55:04.560 --> 00:55:06.670
when the sound is duh.

00:55:06.670 --> 00:55:10.260
So in T, in the word T, they
have three versions of the word

00:55:10.260 --> 00:55:15.960
T, And you know was,
T and T and T and T.

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:17.530
And then they have
the same with D.

00:55:17.530 --> 00:55:21.800
But the register in
which it happens.

00:55:21.800 --> 00:55:26.720
So is it a high register or
a lower register if you will.

00:55:26.720 --> 00:55:34.140
And that goes of course, with
the voicing of the proceeding

00:55:34.140 --> 00:55:34.820
of the constant.

00:55:34.820 --> 00:55:37.580
If you have tuh, which is the
voiceless consonant, which

00:55:37.580 --> 00:55:42.380
is one, that you form by
stiffening your vocal folds,

00:55:42.380 --> 00:55:45.170
you now spread the
stiffening of the vocal folds

00:55:45.170 --> 00:55:47.060
onto the following vowel.

00:55:47.060 --> 00:55:49.945
And therefore the pitch
of the vowel goes up.

00:55:49.945 --> 00:55:51.320
In the other case,
you have slack

00:55:51.320 --> 00:55:53.403
in the vocal folds-- you
have duh, with slackening

00:55:53.403 --> 00:55:56.060
of the vocal folds,
and as a result,

00:55:56.060 --> 00:55:59.040
you spread that over to
the vowel, and as a result,

00:55:59.040 --> 00:56:01.190
the pitch of the vowel drops.

00:56:01.190 --> 00:56:03.560
So in fact, the
rule, there seems

00:56:03.560 --> 00:56:09.230
to be a rule in Chinese, which
said spread that stiffening

00:56:09.230 --> 00:56:12.860
from the preceding consonant
onto the following vowel,

00:56:12.860 --> 00:56:14.090
and that's it.

00:56:14.090 --> 00:56:17.900
So the rule is really,
just exactly like the rule

00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:19.820
that we have for
the English plural,

00:56:19.820 --> 00:56:25.850
only in the English plural,
we spread it among consonants

00:56:25.850 --> 00:56:28.310
and leave the vowels
alone as it were.

00:56:28.310 --> 00:56:32.660
Whereas in Chinese, we spread
from a consonant to the vowel.

00:56:32.660 --> 00:56:35.480
And the results are
exactly as expected.

00:56:35.480 --> 00:56:39.630
But implicit in all this
is, that the gesture,

00:56:39.630 --> 00:56:44.000
the articulately gymnastics,
is the primary thing.

00:56:44.000 --> 00:56:53.740
Whereas the acoustic result is,
as it were a secondary thing.

00:56:53.740 --> 00:56:58.340
So this brings me, I'll just
have one brief conclusion.

00:56:58.340 --> 00:56:59.980
I'll say that I've
argued that words

00:56:59.980 --> 00:57:03.910
are stored in memory of
sequences of discrete phonemes.

00:57:03.910 --> 00:57:05.830
I've suggested that
phonemes are composed

00:57:05.830 --> 00:57:09.380
of more elementary
entities, namely features.

00:57:09.380 --> 00:57:12.170
I've attempted to give some idea
of the nature of the features.

00:57:12.170 --> 00:57:13.830
And I've discussed
a number of factors

00:57:13.830 --> 00:57:17.390
to suggest that a significant
part as an analogy speakers

00:57:17.390 --> 00:57:20.650
have of their language
involves features.

00:57:20.650 --> 00:57:23.200
I noted that because of
its great complexity,

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:25.450
it's implausible to assume
that the machinery involved

00:57:25.450 --> 00:57:27.242
in the memorization
and production of words

00:57:27.242 --> 00:57:28.930
is learned by speakers.

00:57:28.930 --> 00:57:30.880
Instead it's more
plausible to assume

00:57:30.880 --> 00:57:33.040
that much of the knowledge
required for speaking

00:57:33.040 --> 00:57:36.160
is innate, hardwired
in us at birth.

00:57:36.160 --> 00:57:39.220
And finally, I
gave some evidence

00:57:39.220 --> 00:57:41.530
to show that the
articulately reactions

00:57:41.530 --> 00:57:45.160
of the different features
have a more central character

00:57:45.160 --> 00:57:48.887
to the functioning of languages
than their acoustic effects.

00:57:48.887 --> 00:57:51.809
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:00.100 --> 00:58:02.243
Any questions?

00:58:02.243 --> 00:58:06.530
AUDIENCE: If English is
not your [INAUDIBLE],,

00:58:06.530 --> 00:58:10.790
when does the hard
wiring set in?

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:16.080
HALLE: It's different
for different people.

00:58:16.080 --> 00:58:22.610
You know I have a colleague,
Ken Hale, for whom he's 55,

00:58:22.610 --> 00:58:24.380
hardwiring has yet to begin.

00:58:26.960 --> 00:58:28.895
But for most people
it's around puberty.

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:35.830
Puberty, about 12, 13.

00:58:35.830 --> 00:58:38.300
AUDIENCE: How do deaf mutes
ever learn a language?

00:58:38.300 --> 00:58:40.680
It must be very different.

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:44.060
HALLE: Well, notice that
their natural language

00:58:44.060 --> 00:58:46.130
is, of course, for gesture.

00:58:46.130 --> 00:58:49.880
Now you see, what we speak, your
mom always tells you not to,

00:58:49.880 --> 00:58:55.060
but most of us do.

00:58:55.060 --> 00:59:01.250
And, the deaf you
see, they develop

00:59:01.250 --> 00:59:05.180
language which is every
bit as complex as English.

00:59:05.180 --> 00:59:06.680
It's not English.

00:59:06.680 --> 00:59:11.440
I mean deaf, deaf mutes
do not just sign words.

00:59:11.440 --> 00:59:14.870
I mean, they say English
words in the order in which

00:59:14.870 --> 00:59:16.150
the English syntax puts them.

00:59:16.150 --> 00:59:18.815
They have a sign
language, which--

00:59:18.815 --> 00:59:20.940
AUDIENCE: Their memory must
work quite differently.

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:24.830
HALLE: No, their memory
is in terms of gestures.

00:59:24.830 --> 00:59:28.460
And they happen to have
gestures of the upper body

00:59:28.460 --> 00:59:31.370
and the hands,
rather than gestures

00:59:31.370 --> 00:59:35.780
of the lips and
those articulators

00:59:35.780 --> 00:59:39.350
that we use of those
of us who can hear.

00:59:39.350 --> 00:59:44.290
And it's clearly--
and as you well know,

00:59:44.290 --> 00:59:47.420
that these children,
for example,

00:59:47.420 --> 00:59:53.300
deaf children of parents who
are not deaf, they learn sign

00:59:53.300 --> 00:59:56.090
and they learn sign correctly,
although the parents very often

00:59:56.090 --> 00:59:58.728
don't know how to
sign correctly.

01:00:01.640 --> 01:00:04.390
Because a lot of it is
really innate in them.

01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:06.630
And we have these both
parallel channels,

01:00:06.630 --> 01:00:11.390
of which, for obvious
energetic reasons,

01:00:11.390 --> 01:00:17.240
the spoken, the one
that we use for speaking

01:00:17.240 --> 01:00:20.150
is to be preferred,
because you're

01:00:20.150 --> 01:00:22.710
moving much smaller masses.

01:00:22.710 --> 01:00:25.390
You know, when you move
your arms and your body,

01:00:25.390 --> 01:00:27.358
I mean, you're
really moving pounds.

01:00:27.358 --> 01:00:28.900
You know, whereas
when you move this,

01:00:28.900 --> 01:00:30.850
you just move a few grams.

01:00:30.850 --> 01:00:32.980
And therefore it's
much more efficient

01:00:32.980 --> 01:00:34.010
from that point of view.

01:00:34.010 --> 01:00:35.650
So that's the preferred one.

01:00:35.650 --> 01:00:39.730
But sign languages
have existed and exist

01:00:39.730 --> 01:00:44.530
there's just every bit as
complicated as other languages.

01:00:44.530 --> 01:00:45.650
Louis.

01:00:45.650 --> 01:00:51.580
AUDIENCE: The
storing of a language

01:00:51.580 --> 01:00:56.630
involves many thousands of
words and [INAUDIBLE] and so on.

01:00:56.630 --> 01:01:06.000
And it's tempting
at least to, ask,

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:09.220
it is a memory in terms
of all these words, which

01:01:09.220 --> 01:01:15.580
is very large, or is it in
terms of features or whatever,

01:01:15.580 --> 01:01:19.210
and features and rules,
which can combine

01:01:19.210 --> 01:01:21.640
things to make the words.

01:01:21.640 --> 01:01:23.040
What's know about this?

01:01:23.040 --> 01:01:24.600
Or what's speculated?

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:29.020
HALLE: What I showed you that
the representation would have

01:01:29.020 --> 01:01:32.920
to be in terms of some feature.

01:01:32.920 --> 01:01:37.720
I mean we're extremely
gifted, enormously

01:01:37.720 --> 01:01:41.460
gifted for memorizing
enormous numbers of words.

01:01:41.460 --> 01:01:46.000
You know, the average
child aged six,

01:01:46.000 --> 01:01:49.600
knows something like I
don't know 30,000 words.

01:01:49.600 --> 01:01:52.060
And that's an enormous number.

01:01:52.060 --> 01:01:57.110
You know, when you think that, I
mean there's stages in children

01:01:57.110 --> 01:02:01.590
or ever they learn something
like a word an hour,

01:02:01.590 --> 01:02:02.710
a new word an hour.

01:02:02.710 --> 01:02:05.920
Those of you who've taken
language courses and have

01:02:05.920 --> 01:02:09.490
had to memorize the words.

01:02:09.490 --> 01:02:13.820
Well, we all know what a
horrible, difficult task

01:02:13.820 --> 01:02:14.320
that is.

01:02:14.320 --> 01:02:16.900
And so we're very capable,
I mean, naturally,

01:02:16.900 --> 01:02:20.080
very gifted at memorizing
thousands and thousands

01:02:20.080 --> 01:02:24.250
of words at a young age.

01:02:24.250 --> 01:02:27.970
And the rules of language,
like those that I showed you,

01:02:27.970 --> 01:02:31.090
are very obviously
in terms of features.

01:02:31.090 --> 01:02:35.530
A feature changes because
you spread this feature

01:02:35.530 --> 01:02:38.240
onto some adjacent
phoneme, as I tried

01:02:38.240 --> 01:02:42.690
to show you or things like
that, that what goes on.

01:02:42.690 --> 01:02:44.957
Sir.

01:02:44.957 --> 01:02:46.540
AUDIENCE: You make
a good case for how

01:02:46.540 --> 01:02:52.140
[INAUDIBLE] mechanical
question or gymnastic question.

01:02:52.140 --> 01:02:57.030
How do you explain roof, roofs,
and hoof, hooves, or the fact

01:02:57.030 --> 01:02:59.340
that we use a different
plural for the word loaf,

01:02:59.340 --> 01:03:00.720
depending on its meaning?

01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:01.220
HALLE: Yeah.

01:03:07.230 --> 01:03:08.500
You didn't get the handout.

01:03:08.500 --> 01:03:11.310
In number seven, I said
that, or number six,

01:03:11.310 --> 01:03:15.180
